[38;2;124;124;124mperfect: [PSL(2,5), PSL(2,5)] = PSL(2,5). The commutator subgroup [SL(2,5), [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mSL(2,5)] maps surjectively onto [PSL(2,5), PSL(2,5)] = PSL(2,5), so [SL(2,5), [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mSL(2,5)] · Z(SL(2,5)) = SL(2,5). It remains to show −I ∈ [SL(2,5), SL(2,5)].[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mConsider A = [[0,−1],[1,0]] and B = [[2,0],[0,3]] in SL(2,5). Both have [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mdeterminant 1 (det A = 0·0−(−1)·1 = 1, det B = 2·3 = 6 ≡ 1 mod 5). We compute:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- A⁻¹ = [[0,1],[−1,0]], B⁻¹ = [[3,0],[0,2]][0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- AB = [[0,−3],[2,0]] = [[0,2],[2,0]][0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- B⁻¹AB = [[3,0],[0,2]][[0,2],[2,0]] = [[0,1],[4,0]][0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- [A,B] = A⁻¹B⁻¹AB = [[0,1],[−1,0]][[0,1],[4,0]] = [[4,0],[0,−1]] = [[4,0],[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m[0,4]] = −I.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThus −I is a commutator, so Z(SL(2,5)) ⊆ [SL(2,5), SL(2,5)], and therefore [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mSL(2,5) = [SL(2,5), SL(2,5)] is perfect.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 2: SL(2,5) is the Schur cover of A_5.**[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe Schur multiplier of A_5 is M(A_5) = Z/2 (a standard result: M(A_n) ≅ Z/2 for[0m[38;2;124;124;124mn ≥ 5). The Schur cover (universal central extension) of A_5 is the unique [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mperfect central extension of A_5 with kernel M(A_5) ≅ Z/2, hence has order 60 · [0m[38;2;124;124;124m2 = 120.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSince SL(2,5) is a perfect central extension of A_5 with kernel Z/2 (hence order[0m[38;2;124;124;124m120), it must be the Schur cover of A_5.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Step 3: G is a quotient of the Schur cover.**[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow assume G is a perfect group with |G| = 120, |Z(G)| = 2, and G/Z(G) ≅ A_5. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mThen G is a perfect central extension of A_5 with kernel Z(G) ≅ Z/2.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBy the universal property of the Schur cover, there exists a surjective [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mhomomorphism φ: SL(2,5) → G such that the following diagram commutes:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m```[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1 → Z/2 → SL(2,5) → A_5 → 1[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m    ↓ φ|    ↓ φ      ↓ id[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1 → Z/2 →   G     → A_5 → 1[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m```[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe kernel of φ is contained in Z(SL(2,5)) ≅ Z/2. Since |SL(2,5)| = 120 = |G|, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe surjection φ must be an isomorphism.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mTherefore, G ≅ SL(2,5). □[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Remark on the original statement.** Without the assumption that G is perfect, [0m[38;2;124;124;124mthe direct product Z/2 × A_5 also satisfies all stated conditions (|G| = 120, |[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mZ(G)| = 2, G/Z(G) ≅ A_5, G non-solvable). The direct product is not perfect (its[0m[38;2;124;124;124mabelianization is Z/2), so it is excluded by the perfectness assumption. [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mEquivalently, one could assume the extension is non-split (no subgroup of G is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124misomorphic to A_5), which also excludes Z/2 × A_5 and forces G ≅ SL(2,5).[0m

